<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Manifest of the `:phone` module.

  ### What is NOT in it, and this is the most important point of the file

  **No `INTERNET` permission.** By construction of the system the application therefore cannot
  open any socket: immediate `SecurityException`. That is a *verifiable* guarantee — an
  `aapt dump permissions` is enough to check it — where a privacy policy is a promise. A night of
  sleep, a medication dose and a clinical questionnaire leave this device only through the
  explicit export of `export/`, to a location the user chooses via SAF.

  A corollary to hold to over time: **no analytics, crash-reporting or advertising SDK.** They all
  require `INTERNET`; adding one would pull the permission up through manifest merging and
  silently remove the guarantee above.

  `ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE` is not declared either: WorkManager only requires it for network
  constraints, and no worker here sets any.

  ### Backup
  `allowBackup="false"` **and** the two rule files: see the KDoc of
  `res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml` for the reason — D2D transfer is a separate channel that
  `allowBackup` does not cover.

  ### Theme
  `android:theme` is **mandatory here**: without it, Android does not apply a neutral theme but
  `Theme.DeviceDefault.Light.DarkActionBar`, whose light window background shows through
  everywhere the composition does not paint one. See `res/values/themes.xml`, which carries the
  measurement.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Health Connect. `READ_SLEEP` alone is not enough: `SleepFetchWorker` runs by construction
      with the application closed (the provider does not synchronise on waking but when its
      battery policy decides), and without `READ_HEALTH_DATA_IN_BACKGROUND` the read fails
      silently outside the foreground. That is a gap in SPEC v1, corrected here.
      `docs/workings/SLEEP-SOURCES.md` section 6.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_SLEEP" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEALTH_DATA_IN_BACKGROUND" />
    <!--
      Beyond 30 days of history, reading data written by ANOTHER application is refused.
      `RescoreAllWorker` reads the hypnogram of every night again from the raw signal: without this
      permission, a rescore over a campaign longer than a month loses its denominator.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEALTH_DATA_HISTORY" />

    <!--
      Requested on the home screen, but **nothing posts a notification on the phone side yet**: the evening
      reminder is designed (it is a guard rail, not a convenience — people forget to start the
      recording on tired evenings, that is, on evenings correlated with the result) and not
      implemented. The permission is therefore ahead of the feature. If the reminder is abandoned,
      remove this line and the matching button: asking for an unused permission weakens exactly the
      argument of verifiable minimalism the rest of this file makes.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      Package visibility (Android 11+ filters package queries).

      Health Connect, and the two Wear OS companion applications. The latter are the repair for the
      first state of the pairing screen — "no watch paired" is corrected in the manufacturer's
      application, never in a store — and without these declarations
      `getLaunchIntentForPackage` returns `null` even when they are installed. The failure is
      silent: the button does nothing and nothing says why.
    -->
    <queries>
        <package android:name="com.google.android.apps.healthdata" />
        <package android:name="com.google.android.wearable.app" />
        <package android:name="com.samsung.android.app.watchmanager" />
    </queries>

    <application
        android:name="com.pendulum.phone.PendulumApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Pendulum">

        <!--
          Main activity. The CLASS belongs to the package `com.pendulum.phone.ui` and is not
          written here: this manifest only declares it, because an Android module has a single
          manifest.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name="com.pendulum.phone.ui.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Deep link opened **from the watch**, by `RemoteActivityHelper`.

              The API accepts only an `ACTION_VIEW` with a browsable URI, hence `BROWSABLE`. That
              is the constraint of the only path that works: sending a message and calling
              `startActivity` from the `WearableListenerService` has been blocked since Android 10
              — the service runs in the background — and the failure is silent.

              A custom scheme rather than a verified `https` App Link: the latter would require
              hosting an `assetlinks.json`, and therefore depending on a domain, for an application
              that does not even declare the Internet permission. The accepted price is that a
              manufacturer may show an "open with" dialog on first use.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="pendulum" android:host="tonight" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Health Connect permissions rationale — **two mandatory declarations**.

          The `androidx.health.ACTION_SHOW_PERMISSIONS_RATIONALE` path is the one of the Health
          Connect APK (Android 13 and earlier); the `ViewPermissionUsageActivity` alias is the
          framework one (Android 14+, Health Connect built into the system). With `minSdk 30`, both
          halves of the field exist: declaring only one makes Health Connect **refuse to show the
          application** on the other half, and the symptom is "the app does not appear in the
          list", not an error.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name="com.pendulum.phone.health.PermissionsRationaleActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="androidx.health.ACTION_SHOW_PERMISSIONS_RATIONALE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <activity-alias
            android:name="ViewPermissionUsageActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.START_VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE"
            android:targetActivity="com.pendulum.phone.health.PermissionsRationaleActivity">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.HEALTH_PERMISSIONS" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
          Opportunistic trigger for the Health Connect read.

          `ACTION_POWER_CONNECTED` is one of the few broadcasts EXEMPT from the Android 8
          restriction on manifest registration: it still wakes a closed application, which is
          exactly the use case — phone on its dock next to the watch, application not opened since
          the morning.

          The reason is in `FetchSchedule`: the synchronisation from the wrist watch to Health
          Connect is not a random event, it is correlated with usage — watch on the charger, source
          application opened. Exponential backoff alone therefore waits for the T+4 h rung when the
          data arrived at T+2 h 05.

          `exported="false"`: nobody other than the system has any reason to reach it.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="com.pendulum.phone.work.PowerConnectedReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.ACTION_POWER_CONNECTED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          Data Layer reception. `exported="true"` is imposed by the protocol: it is the Google Play
          service that starts this service, including when the application has never been opened —
          which is exactly the use case (the phone receives the chunks during the night, screen
          off).

          **No `android:permission`, and that is measured.** This service long carried
          `android:permission="com.google.android.gms.permission.BIND_WEARABLE_LISTENER"`, and that
          line forbade *every* delivery: the permission is defined by no package, and above all
          Play Services does not **request** it — 384 permissions declared, not that one. An
          install-time permission is granted only to the packages that declare it, so no
          `protectionLevel` repairs anything: defining it ourselves as `signature` and then as
          `signatureOrSystem` was tried, and the refusal is identical down to the character
          (`Permission Denial: ... requires com.google.android.gms.permission.BIND_WEARABLE_LISTENER`).
          The failure mode is silence: the application sees nothing, only the system log carries
          the trace. `docs/workings/BENCH-LOG.md` section 12.1.

          **What protects this service instead, and is a real lock.** The `onBind` of
          `WearableListenerService` is `final` and does not check the caller, but the eleven
          methods of the AIDL interface all go through the same filter, which compares
          `Binder.getCallingUid()` against the UID of the Google Play services and drops the event
          otherwise (`E WearableLS: Caller is not GooglePlayServices`). Measurement made: a call
          issued from a process that is not GMS never reaches `onDataChanged`. To that are added
          the Data Layer, which only carries between the same `applicationId` and the same
          signature, the CRC-32 checked per payload, the `INSERT OR IGNORE`, and the absence of the
          `INTERNET` permission.

          The `DATA_CHANGED` filter is restricted by path prefix: we do not want to be woken by the
          items of another application.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="com.pendulum.phone.ingest.PendulumListenerService"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.DATA_CHANGED" />
                <data
                    android:host="*"
                    android:pathPrefix="/pendulum/"
                    android:scheme="wear" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Fallback when `RemoteActivityHelper` fails on the watch side. The service **does not
              start an activity**: it posts a notification, whose tap is the only reliable exemption
              from the blocking of launches from the background.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.MESSAGE_RECEIVED" />
                <data
                    android:host="*"
                    android:pathPrefix="/pendulum/open-phone"
                    android:scheme="wear" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

    </application>
</manifest>
